title: When a command has finished
tags: Commands
kind: rationale
summary: A command resolves when its effect is visible — the events it caused applied, the panels it shows mounted, the daemon reads it set off ended — so the next command of a sequence acts on what the user would see.


A command is over when ''what it set off'' is over, not when its own code returns.
`panel:open here metarecord-list` returns as soon as the layout is changed; the list
is then built, told it is on screen, and runs its query — and a `cursor:next self`
sent at that moment would move a cursor over the rows of before. Every sequence of
commands meets this: a [[user command|User commands]] invoking several, a script's
[[mf gui command]] calls, a macro.

So a command resolves only once:

# every event the GUI emitted for it is applied — the layout, the workspace variables (the selection a viewer follows);
# a frame has passed, so the panels on screen are placed and told whether they are visible;
# the panels on screen are mounted;
# no daemon read a panel or a command made is in flight — and this stays true for two frames running, since a panel often starts its read a timer after the frame that shows it.

Step 4 is repeated as long as a round finds a read in flight: the answer of one read
may start the next (a list's page, then the viewer following its new cursor).

!! Why one wait for every command

No command says what it sets off, and none needs to: what a command causes on screen
goes through the same places — the events the GUI emits, the panels' mounts, and the
daemon calls, all of which a panel makes through `metafolder.daemon` and the shell
counts (the "working" indicator's tracker). A wait per command would have to be
written, and kept right, for each of some 135 commands; this one cannot forget any.

It waits for ''every'' read in flight, the other workspace's included: it cannot tell
which ones the command caused, and waiting a little too long is harmless where acting
too early is not.

!! Limits

* A panel that reads without end — a poll — would never be quiet: after twenty rounds the command resolves anyway.
* What does not go through `metafolder.daemon` is not waited for: an image or a video the panel is still decoding, a typed text a panel applies after a delay of its own (the [[metarecord-list panel]]'s finder waits for typing to pause).
* The background polls — the change feed, the task list — are not foreground reads and are not waited for: a change written ''outside'' the GUI shows when the feed sees it ([[Daemon data in panels]]).
* A key does not wait for the previous key's command: pressing `j` three times runs three commands at once, as before. Only a sequence that awaits each command — a user command's `mf.invoke`, a script — is ordered by this.

The wait costs two frames at least (about 30 ms) per command, which a script driving
hundreds of commands notices; a key press does not, since nothing waits for it.
